# Dates from engine/notation/chronicle.ts: Boxgrove's hand axes about 480,000 years ago; Bronze Age
# Britain about 2500 to 800 BC; the Iron Age from about 800 BC to the Romans in AD 43.
lesson history-stone-bronze-and-iron v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=1 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Stone, bronze and iron"
  goal "Put tools of stone, bronze and iron in the order people first made them, and read a museum card for when and where a tool was made."

  look {
    scene 34x20 {
      column all at=canvas(0, 0) space=1 align=start {
        objectcard card kind=0 name="Flint hand axe" use="Cutting meat and wood" year=-480000 about=1 place="Boxgrove, England" ask=0
        timeline line from=-3000 to=0 step=500 years=[-2500, -800] labels=["Bronze Age in Britain", "Iron Age in Britain"]
      }
    }
    say """
      For most of the time people have lived, their best tools were stone. This hand axe was chipped
      from flint about 480,000 years ago. Much later people learnt to melt copper and tin together to
      make bronze, which can be poured into a mould and sharpened again. In Britain that was about
      2500 BC. Iron came about 800 BC. BC means before year one, so 2500 BC is longer ago than 800 BC.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.which-age k=0
    }
    show history.which-age k=1 hard-k=2
    show history.tools-in-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.ages-flag k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.find-on-card k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.tools-in-order k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.ages-flag k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    level medium {
      show history.which-age k=2
    }
    level easy {
      show history.tools-in-order k=2 level=medium
    }
    show history.find-on-card k=1 easy-k=1 hard-k=0 level=medium
    level hard {
      show history.tools-in-order k=3 level=medium
      show history.ages-flag k=3 level=medium
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.why-bronze k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.make-a-label
  }
  remember {
    say "First stone, then bronze, then iron. A year BC counts back from year one, so the bigger the number, the longer ago."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that the ages of the past are named after what people made their best tools from,
    and that they came in an order that objects prove: archaeologists find stone tools far older
    than any metal. The way in names the age of a tool from its material; the core orders three
    tools, places the ages on a line of years BC, and reads a museum card. The wrong idea to watch
    for is reading BC years like ordinary ones, so that 800 BC seems longer ago than 2500 BC; say
    "two thousand five hundred years before year one" aloud. If the child is stuck, line up three
    things at home by age. The three-star task is for you to mark: a museum card for the oldest
    thing in your home. England's key stage 2 history begins with "changes in Britain from the
    Stone Age to the Iron Age". Japan's 社会 for grades three and four (the course of study read
    in its 2008 edition) asks children to study 「古くから残る暮らしにかかわる道具，それらを使っていたころの暮らしの様子」,
    old tools and the lives of the people who used them.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example names the Stone Age from a flint axe, the tools to order start from the axe, the empty flag is the Iron Age or the Bronze Age, and the card asks what a tool was for."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the tools come fully muddled, the empty flag is the earliest, the card asks how long before year one a tool was made, and the two-star question asks why iron helped farmers."
  }
}
